The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions, presidential statements and press statements concerning the situation in the Sudan and South Sudan, in particular resolutions 2417 (2018), 2677 (2023), 2772 (2025), 2778 (2025) and 2779 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of the Sudan and South Sudan, and to the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness and regional cooperation,

Recalling the 2018 Revitalized Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict in the Republic of South Sudan (the Revitalized Agreement) and the Roadmap to a Peaceful and Democratic End of the Transition Period, and urging full implementation without delay,

Expressing grave concern at the continuing conflict in the Sudan, including attacks in Darfur and other regions, and at the increasing risk of spillover into South Sudan and the wider region,

Condemning in the strongest terms the shelling by the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) on Abu Shouk camp on 9 May 2025, which killed fourteen civilians, and all attacks against civilians and civilian objects, including medical facilities, schools, markets and humanitarian personnel and assets, by all parties to the conflict,

Gravely concerned by reports of the transfer of weapons, unmanned aerial systems and military materiel through South Sudanese territory to the RSF, including the reported incident on 3 May 2025 at Nyala Airport, and by any external support that fuels the conflict, and recalling the obligation of all Member States to comply with applicable Security Council sanctions and arms embargoes,

Expressing serious concern at the decision of the Government of the Sudan to shut down the Greater Nile Oil Pipeline, including its impact on the economy and people of South Sudan, and calling for the peaceful resolution of outstanding bilateral issues through dialogue,

Deeply concerned by the dire humanitarian situation in both the Sudan and South Sudan, including displacement, food insecurity and obstruction of humanitarian access, and recalling the need to break the cycle among armed conflict, displacement and food insecurity, as recognized in resolution 2417 (2018),

Reaffirming the importance of accountability and transitional justice, and commending the work of the Panel of Experts on the Sudan, the Commission on Human Rights in South Sudan and United Nations entities in documenting violations and abuses,

Emphasizing the importance of the full, equal, safe and meaningful participation of women and the protection of children in all efforts to resolve the conflict, and recalling resolutions 1325 (2000), 1612 (2005) and 2242 (2015),

Welcoming the efforts of the African Union, the Intergovernmental Authority on Development, the Reconstituted Joint Monitoring and Evaluation Commission and regional leaders to promote dialogue and peaceful settlement, and encouraging continued and coordinated engagement,

1. Demands that all parties to the conflict in the Sudan, including the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and the RSF, immediately cease hostilities, facilitate safe and unhindered humanitarian access, and engage in good-faith dialogue towards a lasting ceasefire and a civilian-led political transition;

2. Condemns in the strongest terms all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including the RSF shelling of Abu Shouk camp on 9 May 2025, the attack on 3 May 2025 against a Médecins Sans Frontières hospital, and all attacks against civilians, humanitarian personnel, medical facilities, schools and other civilian objects, and demands that all parties protect civilians and refrain from indiscriminate attacks;

3. Calls upon all Member States and all parties to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity, independence and national unity of the Sudan and South Sudan, and to refrain from any action that could exacerbate tensions or contribute to the escalation of the conflict;

4. Condemns any transfer of weapons, unmanned aerial systems, military materiel or personnel to the parties to the conflict in the Sudan, including reports of drone shipments through South Sudanese territory to the RSF, and calls upon the Government of South Sudan to cease any such support, exercise effective control over its territory and comply with its obligations under the Revitalized Agreement and applicable international law;

5. Urges the Government of the Sudan and the Government of South Sudan to de-escalate tensions, resolve outstanding bilateral issues peacefully and through dialogue, including with respect to the Greater Nile Oil Pipeline, to restore and protect critical economic infrastructure and personnel, and to respect and implement bilateral agreements;

6. Demands that all parties comply with applicable Security Council arms embargoes and sanctions, calls upon all Member States to exercise vigilance and prevent the transfer of arms and military material to parties to the conflict in violation of such measures, and requests the Panel of Experts on the Sudan to continue to report on such transfers and on any external support to the parties;

7. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need, including across conflict lines and borders, and ensure the safety and security of humanitarian personnel and assets, in accordance with resolution 2417 (2018) and international humanitarian law;

8. Emphasizes the need for accountability for all violations and abuses of international humanitarian law and human rights law, calls upon all parties to cooperate with relevant United Nations mechanisms, including the Panel of Experts on the Sudan and the Commission on Human Rights in South Sudan, and urges the continuation of efforts to strengthen national accountability and transitional justice mechanisms;

9. Reaffirms its support for the United Nations Mission in South Sudan (UNMISS) and the United Nations Country Team, and requests the Secretary-General to ensure that United Nations reporting continues to address cross-border military, humanitarian and economic developments, including the impact of the conflict in the Sudan on South Sudan;

10. Welcomes and encourages continued mediation by the African Union, the Intergovernmental Authority on Development and regional partners, and calls upon all parties to engage constructively with these efforts;

11. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within sixty days on the implementation of this resolution, including on cross-border military support, the status of the Greater Nile Oil Pipeline and humanitarian access;

12. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

