1. Demands that all parties to the conflict in the Sudan, including the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF), immediately cease hostilities, agree without delay to a sustained ceasefire, facilitate safe and unhindered humanitarian access, and engage in good-faith dialogue towards an inclusive, Sudanese-led and Sudanese-owned civilian-led political transition, with the full, equal, safe and meaningful participation of women and civil society, in full respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, independence and national unity of the Sudan and with the support of the African Union and the Intergovernmental Authority on Development;

2. Condemns in the strongest terms all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including the RSF shelling of Abu Shouk camp on 9 May 2025, the reported attack on 3 May 2025 against a Médecins Sans Frontières hospital, the March 2025 attack against United Nations personnel in Nasir County, Upper Nile State, and all attacks against civilians, humanitarian personnel, medical facilities, schools and other civilian objects, including conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, grave violations against children and ethnically motivated violence, demands that all parties protect civilians, including women and children, and ensure survivors’ access to services, stresses the need to end impunity for such acts, and underscores that attacks against medical and humanitarian personnel may constitute war crimes;

3. Calls upon all Member States and all parties to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity, independence and national unity of the Sudan and South Sudan, and to refrain from any action that could exacerbate tensions or contribute to the escalation of the conflict;

4. Takes note with concern of reports of transfers of weapons, unmanned aerial systems, military materiel or personnel to parties to the conflict in the Sudan, calls for these reports to be verified through appropriate existing United Nations mechanisms, and calls upon all Member States and all parties to refrain from any action that could exacerbate tensions or contribute to the escalation of the conflict, to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity, independence and national unity of the Sudan and South Sudan, and to comply with applicable Security Council sanctions and arms embargoes;

5. Urges the Government of the Sudan and the Government of South Sudan to de-escalate tensions and resolve outstanding bilateral issues peacefully and through dialogue, with the good offices of the Intergovernmental Authority on Development and the African Union, including with respect to the Greater Nile Oil Pipeline; calls upon the Government of the Sudan to urgently reconsider the shutdown of the Greater Nile Oil Pipeline and to avoid measures that exacerbate the humanitarian and economic situation of the civilian population in South Sudan; and calls upon both Governments to restore and protect critical economic infrastructure and personnel and to respect and implement bilateral agreements;

6. Demands that all parties comply with applicable Security Council arms embargoes and sanctions, calls upon all Member States to exercise vigilance and prevent the transfer of arms and military materiel to parties to the conflict in violation of such measures, recalls that applicable arms embargoes should be implemented in a manner that does not impede the implementation of the Revitalized Agreement, including the equipping and deployment of the Necessary Unified Forces, encourages Member States to report alleged violations to the Security Council Committee established pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005), and requests the Secretary-General to continue reporting on cross-border military and economic developments through existing reporting channels, in consultation with the Governments of the Sudan and South Sudan;

7. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate safe, rapid, sustained and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need, including refugees, returnees and host communities, including across conflict lines and borders and to Darfur, Kordofan and other conflict-affected areas, including through the Adré crossing and other available corridors; calls for the removal of arbitrary impediments, illegal fees and taxes that obstruct humanitarian delivery; and demands that all parties ensure the safety and security of humanitarian personnel and assets, in accordance with resolution 2417 (2018) and international humanitarian law;

8. Emphasizes the need for accountability for all violations and abuses of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence and grave violations against children, recalls that such violations may amount to international crimes, including war crimes and crimes against humanity, calls upon all parties to cooperate with relevant United Nations and national mechanisms, as appropriate, and urges the continuation of efforts to strengthen national accountability and transitional justice mechanisms, with full respect for national ownership;

9. Reaffirms its support for the United Nations Mission in South Sudan (UNMISS) and the United Nations Country Team; stresses the need for full and unhindered freedom of movement and that UNMISS operates without political interference or obstruction in implementing its mandate, including protection of civilians, human rights monitoring and support for humanitarian access; and requests the Secretary-General to ensure that United Nations reporting continues, consistent with the mandate of UNMISS and in coordination with the host Government and in consultation with the Governments of South Sudan and the Sudan, to address relevant cross-border humanitarian, military and economic developments, including the impact of the conflict in the Sudan on South Sudan;

10. Reaffirms its support for the full implementation of the Revitalized Agreement and the Roadmap, and calls upon the transitional government of South Sudan and all parties to accelerate implementation, including on unified security forces, transitional justice and preparations for free, fair and inclusive elections in December 2026, with the full, equal, safe and meaningful participation of women and youth;

11. Calls for the full, equal, safe and meaningful participation of women in all peace and political processes, including ceasefire and transition arrangements, and for the protection of children from all grave violations, including recruitment and use;

12. Welcomes and encourages continued mediation by the African Union, the Intergovernmental Authority on Development and regional partners, as well as the good offices of the Secretary-General’s Personal Envoy for the Sudan, in accordance with the principle of African solutions to African problems and with full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Sudan and South Sudan;

13. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within sixty days, and every ninety days thereafter, on the implementation of this resolution, including on cross-border humanitarian and economic developments, the status of the Greater Nile Oil Pipeline and humanitarian access;

14. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

